    "case_id": "150",
    "case_summary": "A woman in her 50s presented with bilateral nasal obstruction, thick nasal discharge, and facial pain and pressure. Her condition was recalcitrant to 3 prior surgeries, immunotherapy, and medical therapy.\n\nInitial post-procedure examination showed:- Binocular horizontal diplopia with left lateral gaze- Inability for the left eye to track beyond the midline- Left eye was not proptotic, ecchymotic, or tense- Bedside intraocular pressure measurements: 15 OD, 16 OS (normal)- National Institutes of Health stroke score of 1\n\n{'Clinical Images': {'Figure 1': {'Title': 'Clinical Images', 'Description': 'Images showing left-sided abducens nerve palsy immediately following surgery and its resolution by postoperative day 1'}, 'Figure 2': {'Title': 'Illustrative Representations of the Proposed Mechanisms', 'Description': 'Diagrams showing different hypotheses for abducens nerve palsy mechanisms following sphenopalatine ganglion blockade'}}, 'Emergency Evaluation': 'Workup results were negative for a cerebrovascular accident'}\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Allergic fungal rhinosinusitis\nB. Transient left abducens (cranial nerve VI) nerve palsy\nC. Chronic rhinosinusitis with nasal polyps\nD. Iatrogenic Abducens Nerve Palsy\n",
